WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.284 --> 00:00:06.225
Hello, everyone!

2
00:00:06.245 --> 00:00:23.072
I'm Faye Welcome to another episode of Superstars in History, the talk show featuring interviews with famous people

3
00:00:23.212 --> 00:00:24.472
from the past.

4
00:00:25.353 --> 00:00:32.876
Today's guest was a lawyer and a political fighter who used nonviolence to help India, then a British

5
00:00:32.936 --> 00:00:36.254
colony, become an independent country.

6
00:00:37.415 --> 00:00:44.241
Known as the Mahatma—or Great Soul—please welcome Mohandas Gandhi!

7
00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:05.121
Faye. Please call me Gandhi.

8
00:01:05.981 --> 00:01:09.463
The Mahatma title always made me uncomfortable.

9
00:01:10.563 --> 00:01:12.324
You are so modest!

10
00:01:12.704 --> 00:01:17.026
And I know you were also very shy, especially as a child.

11
00:01:17.886 --> 00:01:20.047
What else can you tell us about your early life?

12
00:01:21.128 --> 00:01:28.351
I was born in Porbandar, India, in 1869, when India was part of the British Empire.

13
00:01:29.471 --> 00:01:35.206
My family was Hindu, and my mother was very devout and fasted regularly.

14
00:01:36.147 --> 00:01:40.649
My father was a local government leader, and we lived comfortably.

15
00:01:41.830 --> 00:01:47.373
India, however, was a poor country divided by caste and religion.

16
00:01:48.393 --> 00:01:54.997
There were many poor people and untouchables—outcasts who performed the dirtiest jobs.

17
00:01:56.117 --> 00:02:00.740
India had a large Hindu population, but there were also many Muslims.

18
00:02:01.580 --> 00:02:05.882
Though the two groups didn't always get along, I had Muslim friends.

19
00:02:06.883 --> 00:02:13.867
In fact, even though I was a vegetarian for religious reasons, I tried eating meat with a brave

20
00:02:13.947 --> 00:02:17.529
and strong Muslim friend, hoping to be more like him!

21
00:02:18.830 --> 00:02:20.931
Alas, it didn't work.

22
00:02:22.131 --> 00:02:25.653
At age 19 you went to London to study law.

23
00:02:26.494 --> 00:02:28.655
By then you were married and had a son.

24
00:02:30.688 --> 00:02:35.151
Yes, our parents had arranged the marriage, which was customary then.

25
00:02:36.191 --> 00:02:39.913
My wife and son stayed in India while I was away.

26
00:02:40.974 --> 00:02:49.499
Three years later I returned to India to practice law, but because of my shy personality, I had

27
00:02:49.579 --> 00:02:52.280
little success.

28
00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:58.684
In 1893 I was offered a position in South Africa, which was also part of the British Empire.

29
00:02:59.748 --> 00:03:06.974
I moved there with my family for a yearlong job, but God led me to stay there for

30
00:03:06.994 --> 00:03:08.035
21 years.

31
00:03:09.216 --> 00:03:10.137
What was the job?

32
00:03:11.158 --> 00:03:14.801
I performed legal services for Indian immigrants.

33
00:03:15.922 --> 00:03:23.989
Many Indians had immigrated there for work, and we encountered unfair treatment from the white settlers who ruled

34
00:03:24.069 --> 00:03:24.949
South Africa.

35
00:03:25.850 --> 00:03:29.781
We had to pay an annual tax, which many couldn't afford.

36
00:03:30.742 --> 00:03:35.304
We also had to be fingerprinted and carry registration cards.

37
00:03:36.405 --> 00:03:41.247
One of your firsthand experiences with discrimination was a turning point in your life.

38
00:03:42.588 --> 00:03:49.872
Yes. I was on a train in a first-class compartment when a white man did not want to

39
00:03:49.952 --> 00:03:50.712
sit near me.

40
00:03:51.673 --> 00:03:54.955
The railway officials told me to move to another compartment.

41
00:03:55.875 --> 00:03:59.273
When I refused, they forced me off the train.

42
00:04:02.514 --> 00:04:09.437
I spent the night in a cold train station, thinking about the deep disease of prejudice.

43
00:04:10.718 --> 00:04:16.120
I decided then to fight it, even if I had to suffer hardship in the process.

44
00:04:17.260 --> 00:04:26.064
I began organizing workers' strikes and protests, and encouraged civil disobedience to protest unfair laws.

45
00:04:27.092 --> 00:04:30.535
Why was nonviolent protest so important to you?

46
00:04:31.616 --> 00:04:39.403
Hindus believe in nonviolence, and I had read religious texts and writings from great thinkers that confirmed to

47
00:04:39.503 --> 00:04:43.766
me that nonviolence was the best way to fight oppression.

48
00:04:44.747 --> 00:04:49.671
I called this satyagraha, which means "force of the soul."

49
00:04:50.632 --> 00:04:54.956
We didn't hate our oppressors; we wanted to win them over.

50
00:04:56.052 --> 00:04:57.313
Your campaign worked!

51
00:04:58.194 --> 00:05:02.818
Those unjust laws against Indians in South Africa were changed.

52
00:05:03.758 --> 00:05:04.899
What did you do next?

53
00:05:05.920 --> 00:05:10.164
My family and I returned to India in 1915.

54
00:05:11.084 --> 00:05:18.450
But my experiences with discrimination in South Africa led me to question more than ever the role of

55
00:05:18.510 --> 00:05:20.252
the British in my homeland.

56
00:05:21.292 --> 00:05:27.496
How could 100,000 British rule 315 million Indians?

57
00:05:28.696 --> 00:05:32.799
You had some very specific goals to prepare for independence, didn't you?

58
00:05:34.039 --> 00:05:41.944
I wanted Indians to become more self-reliant, growing our own food and spinning our own clothes.

59
00:05:43.064 --> 00:05:49.488
I also wanted to improve the lives of the poor and end discrimination against untouchables.

60
00:05:50.692 --> 00:05:57.655
After all, how could we expect the English to stop oppressing us when we Indians oppressed untouchables?

61
00:05:58.655 --> 00:06:02.017
And I preached unity between Hindus and Muslims.

62
00:06:03.077 --> 00:06:06.999
We had to be united if we were to rule ourselves!

63
00:06:08.119 --> 00:06:11.941
Peaceful protests also played a big part in your strategy.

64
00:06:12.921 --> 00:06:19.163
Yes, but sadly, horrible violence broke out in Amritsar in 1919.

65
00:06:21.264 --> 00:06:26.028
A British general ordered soldiers to fire on a peaceful protest.

66
00:06:27.149 --> 00:06:30.912
Hundreds were killed, and a thousand were wounded.

67
00:06:31.893 --> 00:06:37.998
Then, to my horror, Indians became violent, killing some police officers.

68
00:06:39.039 --> 00:06:42.182
To protest this violence, I fasted.

69
00:06:43.222 --> 00:06:46.705
Fasting wasn't a tactic I could use against my enemies.

70
00:06:47.686 --> 00:06:54.774
It only worked on my supporters, who worried so much when I fasted that they changed their behavior

71
00:06:54.854 --> 00:06:55.494
for the good.

72
00:06:56.535 --> 00:07:01.117
They feared I would die—and sometimes I almost did.

73
00:07:02.097 --> 00:07:06.199
I fasted often—my longest fast was three weeks.

74
00:07:07.520 --> 00:07:11.702
Your most famous protest was the Salt March of 1930.

75
00:07:12.662 --> 00:07:14.203
Can you tell us more about that?

76
00:07:15.043 --> 00:07:22.809
The British taxed salt, so I organized a 24-day, 240-mile march to the sea.

77
00:07:24.030 --> 00:07:30.456
Once there, we gathered our own salt to avoid the tax, an act that broke the law.

78
00:07:31.397 --> 00:07:34.740
Thousands of us, including me, were arrested.

79
00:07:35.741 --> 00:07:43.228
But importantly, the British realized that they couldn't rule India without the cooperation of the Indian people.

80
00:07:44.224 --> 00:07:50.609
The British held a conference on the situation in India, and I traveled to London to participate.

81
00:07:51.590 --> 00:07:52.431
How did that go?

82
00:07:53.551 --> 00:07:58.635
I'm afraid I returned to India without an agreement on my country's independence.

83
00:07:59.736 --> 00:08:05.521
When World War II started, Britain drew India into the fighting without our consent.

84
00:08:06.682 --> 00:08:13.561
I started the "Quit India" campaign, encouraging Indians to act as if we were no longer under British

85
00:08:13.641 --> 00:08:19.543
rule. I was arrested again, along with many other Indian leaders.

86
00:08:20.643 --> 00:08:22.684
Two years later I was released.

87
00:08:23.644 --> 00:08:28.005
Nearly six years of my life were spent in Indian jails.

88
00:08:29.105 --> 00:08:34.367
When the war ended, the British were finally ready to give India its independence!

89
00:08:35.687 --> 00:08:37.888
It wasn't so simple though.

90
00:08:38.868 --> 00:08:45.794
The Muslim minority didn't trust the Hindu majority to rule them fairly, so they wanted their own state

91
00:08:45.834 --> 00:08:46.715
of Pakistan.

92
00:08:47.776 --> 00:08:57.043
When India gained independence on August 15, 1947, the country was partitioned into India and Pakistan.

93
00:08:58.084 --> 00:09:00.026
Independence was bittersweet.

94
00:09:01.087 --> 00:09:03.308
I had failed to keep India united!

95
00:09:04.189 --> 00:09:07.432
And the ensuing crisis was dreadful!

96
00:09:08.556 --> 00:09:10.237
I can tell the rest of that story.

97
00:09:11.138 --> 00:09:13.139
Thank you so much for joining us!

98
00:09:14.180 --> 00:09:16.421
We'll let you get back to history now.

99
00:09:17.502 --> 00:09:18.923
Thank you, Faye.

100
00:09:23.046 --> 00:09:29.670
After the partition, Muslims in India moved to Pakistan, and Hindus in Pakistan moved to India.

101
00:09:30.551 --> 00:09:38.501
When horrible violence broke out, Gandhi held prayer meetings with Hindus and Muslims, vowing to fast until death.

102
00:09:39.662 --> 00:09:41.243
Luckily the violence stopped.

103
00:09:42.123 --> 00:09:47.428
But on January 30, 1948, another act of violence occurred.

104
00:09:48.048 --> 00:09:54.693
Gandhi was assassinated by a man upset over Gandhi's support for peaceful Hindu-Muslim relations.

105
00:09:55.654 --> 00:09:56.875
The Mahatma was 78.

106
00:09:58.880 --> 00:10:04.105
Gandhi's nonviolent protests inspired other civil rights leaders such as Dr.

107
00:10:04.145 --> 00:10:06.467
Martin Luther King and Nelson Mandela.

108
00:10:07.368 --> 00:10:13.853
The International Day of Nonviolence is celebrated each year on his birthday, October 2.

109
00:10:14.694 --> 00:10:16.195
That's all for today's show.

110
00:10:16.716 --> 00:10:18.317
I'll see you again next time!
